In this lesson, we'll look at distance, time, and constant speed. The big idea is that these three things are linked by one simple relationship: distance equals speed multiplied by time. You can remember it with the triangle trick — cover up whatever you're trying to find, and whatever's left shows you the formula. If you need the distance, multiply speed by time. If you need the speed, divide distance by time. And if you need the time, divide distance by speed. When you're setting up a problem, first identify what you know and what you're solving for, then pick the form of the formula that puts the unknown on its own. Next, make sure your units match — kilometres with kilometres per hour with hours, or metres with metres per second with seconds. Finally, calculate and check the answer makes sense, because a car travelling a thousand kilometres in five minutes should raise a flag. To write a model, just say something like, let d be the distance in kilometres, and the car travels at eighty kilometres per hour for t hours, so d equals eighty t. Now let's see it in action.